From ed72d28924daf47dc6895195f8cfcae5a7f6cd3a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Valery Petrov <valerypetrov@icloud.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 04:15:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Check a shard that is this server itself on the caller's
 context, not the cluster's credentials

The shard-target check probed every replica through the cluster's connection
pools, so a replica that is this server itself was asked as the cluster
address's user. That is stricter than the operation it models: both PromQL
paths pin prefer_localhost_replica on (and the reads parallel replicas off),
so such a shard is read and written in-process on the caller's context, and
the cluster's credentials need no rights on its table at all. A cluster
entry whose user cannot read system.tables therefore failed the check with
ACCESS_DENIED while the read and the write themselves would have succeeded.

A local replica is now resolved in-process, on the caller's context, and
judged from the storage's own engine and `time_series` column type; only a
genuinely remote replica is still asked over a connection. The verdicts and
messages are unchanged. Because the check resolves that table on the query
context, the write that follows resolves the same table, so the samples go
where they were checked even if the name is exchanged meanwhile.

Since the check now reads that table's metadata, the write asks for the
sink's own INSERT grant on it first, with the columns the sink sends, as the
read already asked for SELECT; both use the non-throwing resolution, so a
name that resolves to nothing stays a refusal the probe reports with a
retryable status rather than a 404 that would have Prometheus drop the batch.

Also fixes the two CI failures of the previous commit: setSetting is
ambiguous between its String and Field overloads for a bare literal.

